Issue Recap

Catalogue pod health endpoint showed:

{"app":"OK", "mongo": false}


Pod was running, but could not connect to MongoDB.

Root Causes

DNS resolution issues – CoreDNS queries failing from some pods.

Networking issues – Some worker nodes couldn’t communicate with other pods due to AWS VPC CNI or SG restrictions.

Worker node Security Group – Missing SG rules to allow pod-to-pod traffic inside the cluster.

Exact Steps We Followed
Step 1: Verify CoreDNS
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
kubectl -n kube-system describe configmap coredns


Confirmed CoreDNS pods were Running and config was correct.

Step 2: Check pod DNS resolution
kubectl run debug --rm -it --image=busybox -- /bin/sh
cat /etc/resolv.conf
nslookup kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local
nslookup catalogue.roboshop.svc.cluster.local


Initially, DNS resolution failed intermittently.

After redeploying debug pod, DNS resolution worked.

Step 3: Verify AWS CNI (Networking)
kubectl get daemonset aws-node -n kube-system -o wide
kubectl -n kube-system logs -l k8s-app=aws-node


All aws-node pods were running and no IP allocation issues were found.

Step 4: Update Worker Node Security Group

Identify worker node SG:

aws ec2 describe-instances \
  --filters "Name=tag:eks:cluster-name,Values=roboshop-dev" \
  --query "Reservations[*].Instances[*].SecurityGroups[*].GroupId" \
  --output text


Describe SG to check rules:

aws ec2 describe-security-groups --group-ids <worker-node-sg-id>


Add inbound rules to allow all traffic from the same SG:

Protocol: All

Port Range: All

Source: <worker-node-sg-id>

✅ This allows pod-to-pod traffic across worker nodes, essential for DNS and MongoDB connectivity.

Step 5: Test connectivity to MongoDB
kubectl exec -it debug -- nslookup mongodb.roboshop.svc.cluster.local
kubectl exec -it debug -- ping <mongodb-pod-ip>
kubectl exec -it debug -- telnet mongodb 27017


Verified debug pod can reach MongoDB via service name and direct pod IP.

Step 6: Confirm Catalogue Pod Health
curl http://catalogue:8080/health


Now returns:

{"app":"OK", "mongo": true}


✅ Catalogue can now connect to MongoDB successfully.

Summary of Fixes
Step	Action
1	Verified CoreDNS pods and config.
2	Checked pod DNS resolution.
3	Verified AWS CNI (aws-node) daemonset.
4	Updated worker node security group to allow all inbound traffic from itself.
5	Tested connectivity from debug pod to MongoDB.
6	Confirmed catalogue health endpoint shows mongo: true.